      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a detailed internal engineering release plan for shipping a new authentication flow in a SaaS product. They want it written as an internal engineering document, not a sales/marketing document. Let me think about what makes this genuinely useful as an internal engineering plan.\n\nKey sections requested:\n1. Feature flags\n2. Staged rollout\n3. Migration steps\n4. Monitoring\n5. Rollback criteria\n6. Customer communication\n7. Security review\n8. Post-release verification\n\nI need to write this as if I'm an engineering lead or platform engineer writing this for the team. It should be specific, actionable, reference real infrastructure concerns, have concrete thresholds, and feel like something you'd actually see in a Confluence page or internal wiki. Not generic fluff.\n\nLet me think about what a real auth flow migration looks like:\n- Probably moving from session-based to token-based (JWT), or adding MFA, or migrating to a new IdP, or implementing OAuth 2.0 / OIDC\n- I'll frame it as: migrating from legacy session-cookie auth to a new token-based flow with OIDC support, MFA, and session management improvements\n- Feature flags: per-tenant, per-region, percentage-based\n- Staged rollout: internal \u2192 dogfooding \u2192 small external cohort \u2192 percentage ramp \u2192 full\n- Migration: dual-running old and new, data migration for sessions, token store changes\n- Monitoring: auth success/failure rates, latency, token validation errors, session store health\n- Rollback: specific SLO breaches, error rate thresholds\n- Customer communication: in-app banners, email, changelog, support macros\n- Security review: threat model, pen test, key rotation, audit logging\n- Post-release: verification checklists, soak period, cleanup\n\nLet me make this detailed, specific, and feel like a real internal doc. I'll use concrete numbers, specific service names (generic but realistic), and operational details.\n</think>\n\n# Release Plan: Auth v2 \u2014 Token-Based Flow with OIDC & MFA\n\n**Owner:** Platform / Identity Team\n**Reviewers:** Security, SRE, Frontend, Backend, Support\n**Target date:** 2025-07-14 (Mon) \u2014 internal GA\n**Status:** DRAFT v0.3 \u2014 pending security sign-off\n\n---\n\n## 0. Scope & Context\n\nWe are replacing the legacy session-cookie auth path (`/",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a detailed internal engineering release plan for shipping a new authentication flow in a SaaS product. They want it written as an internal engineering document, not a sales/marketing document. Let me think about what makes this genuinely useful as an internal engineering plan.\n\nKey sections req",
