      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis phenomenon\u2014high throughput on repeated prompts versus low throughput on unique prompts\u2014is a classic indicator of **inference caching** or **KV-cache reuse**, rather than a reflection of the model\u2019s raw computational speed. Below is a structured analysis of the causes, methods to isolate true performance, and best practices for benchmarking.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Plausible Causes\n\n#### A. KV-Cache Reuse (Most Likely)\nModern LLM inference engines (e.g., vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM) use **Key-Value (KV) caches** to store intermediate attention states.\n- **Repeated Prompts**: If the same prompt is sent multiple times, the engine may reuse the pre-computed KV cache from the first inference. This skips the expensive **prefill** phase (processing the input tokens) and only performs the **decode** phase (generating output tokens). Since prefill is computationally intensive (O(n\u00b2) attention), skipping it drastically increases tokens/sec.\n- **Unique Prompts**: Each new prompt requires a full prefill computation, which is slower per token, especially for long contexts.\n\n#### B. Prompt Caching / Semantic Caching\nSome serving frameworks implement **semantic caching** or **exact-match caching**:\n- If the prompt (or a prefix of it) matches a previously seen prompt, the system may return a cached response or skip computation entirely.\n- This is common in production systems to reduce latency and cost but invalidates throughput benchmarks if not disabled.\n\n#### C. Batch Size and Concurrency Effects\n- **Repeated Prompts**: If the benchmark sends identical prompts in a batch, the engine may optimize memory access patterns or use **continuous batching** more efficiently.\n- **Unique Prompts**: Diverse prompts may lead to less efficient memory coalescing or higher variance in sequence lengths, reducing effective batch efficiency.\n\n#### D. Warm-Up vs. Cold Start\n- **First Request**: Includes model loading, CUDA context initialization, and memory allocation.\n- **Subsequent Requests**: Benefit from warmed-up GPU memory and optimized kernels.\n- If the benchmark does not properly separate warm-up from steady-state, repeated prompts may appear faster due to accumulated warm-up benefits.\n\n---\n\n### 2. How to Separate Valid Fresh-Response Throughput from Warmed/Cached Throughput\n\nTo measure **true inference performance**, you must ensure that every request is treated as a **cold, unique computation**.\n\n",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis phenomenon\u2014high throughput on repeated prompts versus low throughput on unique prompts\u2014is a classic indicator of **inference caching** or **KV-cache reuse**, rather than a reflection of the model\u2019s raw computational speed. Below is a structured analysis of the causes, methods to isolate true p",
