      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking for a detailed, expert-level analysis of GPU inference kernel profiling for a Mixture of Experts (MoE) model during the decode phase. They want performance hypotheses to test across 8 specific dimensions. This is a systems/ML engineering question that requires deep knowledge of:\n\n1. MoE architecture (gate, up, down projections, expert routing)\n2. GPU kernel profiling (Nsight, CUPTI, etc.)\n3. Kernel fusion strategies\n4. Quantized matmul (FP8, INT8, etc.) and memory layout\n5. Expert routing and caching\n6. CUDA graph capture\n7. Batching and shape considerations\n8. Memory bandwidth analysis\n9. Synchronization overhead\n10. Validation pitfalls (correctness vs performance traps)\n\nLet me structure this as a comprehensive set of testable hypotheses, each with:\n- The hypothesis statement\n- Why it matters (mechanism)\n- How to test it (concrete experiment)\n- What to look for (expected signal)\n- Pitfalls / validation traps\n\nI'll make this thorough, technically precise, and organized. Let me think through each dimension carefully.\n\n**MoE Gate/Up/Down Projections in Decode:**\n- In decode (batch=1 or small batch), the MoE layer has:\n  - Gate projection: projects hidden state to compute expert scores (small matmul)\n  - Router: selects top-k experts\n  - Up projection: projects hidden state to intermediate (per expert)\n  - Down projection: projects intermediate back to hidden (per expert)\n- The key issue: in decode, each token only activates top-k experts (e.g., 2 out of 8 or 16), so the matmuls are very thin (M=1 or small M, N=intermediate_dim, K=hidden_dim)\n- This makes them memory-bandwidth bound rather than compute bound\n- The gate/up/down projections dominate because they're the bulk of the FLOPs per token in MoE layers\n\nLet me write a comprehensive, well-structured response.\n</think>\n\n# Performance Hypotheses for MoE Gate/Up/Down Projections in Decode\n\n## Context & Framing\n\nIn the decode phase (autoregressive, batch size 1\u20138), each MoE layer executes a **gate projection** (hidden \u2192 expert scores), a **top-k router**, then **up** and **down",
